<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alawites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nusayris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alawites are a religious community that emerged from Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the 9th and 10th centuries, named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Ali and associated with the teacher Ibn Nusayr, hence the older name Nusayris. Concentrated in the coastal mountains of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and around Antakya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, they were long marginalised under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Under the French Mandate they had their own Alawite State, and from 1970 the Alawite Assad family ruled Syria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alawites.jpg" alt="Alawites" width="320" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frank Hurley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Celebrating_Alawites.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
